The top of a stack is the first value we can read. Putting values into a queue records that pop order. Reading the queue from front to back and pushing each value reverses the stack.

This uses the required `stack<int>` directly. The input stack is emptied during the process and then filled again.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <stack>

class Solution {
public:
    void reverseStack(std::stack<int> &st) {
        std::queue<int> values;

        while (!st.empty()) {
            values.push(st.top());
            st.pop();
        }

        while (!values.empty()) {
            st.push(values.front());
            values.pop();
        }
    }
};
```

Suppose the stack has `5` at the bottom and `8` at the top. The queue becomes `8, 5`, and pushing those values back makes `8` the bottom and `5` the top.

Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(n) for the queue.

A queue makes the order change easy. First, all stack values are removed and placed in the queue. The first queue value is the old top, so pushing queue values back creates the reversed stack.

With stack values `1, 2, 3` from bottom to top, the queue receives `3, 2, 1`. Pushing them in that order leaves `3` at the bottom and `1` at the top.

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.Stack;

class Solution {
    public void reverseStack(Stack<Integer> st) {
        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        while (!st.isEmpty()) {
            queue.offer(st.pop());
        }

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            st.push(queue.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

No recursion is needed here. The queue uses extra memory, but each value is moved only twice.

Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(n).

Since the JavaScript stack is an array, its two ends can be swapped directly. The first item is exchanged with the last item, then the next pair is handled until the middle is reached.

This keeps the original array object and changes its contents in place. It also avoids recursion and any second stack.

```javascript
class Solution {
    reverseStack(stack) {
        let left = 0;
        let right = stack.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            const saved = stack[left];
            stack[left] = stack[right];
            stack[right] = saved;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

For `[4, 7, 9, 2]`, the first swap gives `[2, 7, 9, 4]`, and the second gives `[2, 9, 7, 4]`. An empty array needs no special work, and a single item stays unchanged.

Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(1), not counting the input array.

`Stack<int>.ToArray()` returns values from the top toward the bottom. That is already the order needed for rebuilding the reversed stack. After clearing the original stack, pushing the snapshot from left to right puts the old top at the bottom.

The stack is modified in place. The empty case also works because the snapshot simply has length zero.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution
{
    public void ReverseStack(Stack<int> st)
    {
        int[] values = st.ToArray();
        st.Clear();

        for (int i = 0; i < values.Length; i++)
        {
            st.Push(values[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

For a stack with bottom-to-top order `10, 20, 30`, `ToArray()` gives `30, 20, 10`. Pushing that sequence produces bottom-to-top order `30, 20, 10`.

Time complexity: O(n). Space complexity: O(n) for the snapshot.
